diagnosis_text:
No fault exists in this network. A thorough end-to-end investigation was
performed and every component in the alleged failure path is operating
correctly.

host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2/24) has its default gateway correctly set to
10.1.1.1, which is the br0 interface of switch_dist_1_1. That device holds
a full OSPF adjacency (Full/Backup) with router_core_1 over the
172.16.0.6/31 link, and carries a correctly-learned OSPF route to
10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6. router_core_1 in turn holds Full adjacencies
with both router_core_2 and router_core_3, and its routing table shows
10.200.0.0/24 reachable via router_core_3 (172.16.0.3) at OSPF cost 30.
switch_server_access has 10.200.0.0/24 directly connected on br0 (gateway
10.200.0.1), and all four web servers plus the DNS server (10.200.0.2–
10.200.0.6) have default routes pointing back to that gateway. OSPF running
configs match startup configs on every FRR device — there is no injected
static route, route filter, or misconfigured network statement.

Direct confirmation: pings from host_1_1_1_1 to all five server-farm
addresses (10.200.0.2 through 10.200.0.6) completed with 0% packet loss and
consistent sub-2 ms RTTs. The end-to-end TTL of 60 confirms exactly the
expected four-hop path (host → switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 →
router_core_3 → switch_server_access).

Root cause: The helpdesk ticket describes a fault that does not exist — the
network is fully healthy and the symptom cannot be reproduced.

Corrective action: No routing or interface change is needed. The ticket
should be closed as a false alarm; if the user's own machine showed symptoms
(browser error, stale ARP, local firewall), that should be investigated on
the end-user workstation rather than in the network infrastructure.
=== END ===
